window.API_CARDS = [{"n":0.1,"ti":"Python for Backend Work","f":"p1-python-for-backend.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"What does plain Python do with def f(x: int) when you call f(\"hello\") ?","o":["Raises a TypeError before the function runs","Converts \"hello\" to an int","Nothing special: the hint is not enforced at run time","Refuses to compile the file"],"a":2,"w":"Hints are for tools such as type checkers and frameworks. Validation needs something like Pydantic."},{"n":0.1,"ti":"Python for Backend Work","f":"p1-python-for-backend.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"You run python app/main.py from the project root. What is sys.path[0] ?","o":["The app/ folder that contains main.py","The project root","The Python installation folder","An empty string"],"a":0,"w":"Running a file directly puts that file's folder first, which is why import app then fails."},{"n":0.1,"ti":"Python for Backend Work","f":"p1-python-for-backend.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"Which error appears when a file using from .services import orders is run directly as a script?","o":["ModuleNotFoundError: No module named 'services'","SyntaxError: invalid import","It works, because the services folder is next to it","ImportError: attempted relative import with no known parent package"],"a":3,"w":"A file run directly is the top-level __main__ module with no package, so there is nothing for the dot to be relative to."},{"n":0.1,"ti":"Python for Backend Work","f":"p1-python-for-backend.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"What does Pydantic (default lax mode) do with quantity: int given the string \"3\" ?","o":["Rejects it, because it is a string","Converts it to the integer 3","Stores the string unchanged","Converts it to the float 3.0"],"a":1,"w":"Lax mode converts unambiguous values. \"three\" or 3.5 would be rejected; strict mode would reject \"3\" as well."},{"n":0.1,"ti":"Python for Backend Work","f":"p1-python-for-backend.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"Why use python -m pip install rather than pip install ?","o":["It installs faster","It also creates a lock file","It installs into the Python interpreter you are actually running","It checks package signatures"],"a":2,"w":"A bare pip on the PATH may belong to a different Python than the one running your service."},{"n":0.2,"ti":"Async Python & the Event Loop","f":"p2-async-python.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"What does calling an async def function, without awaiting it, do?","o":["Runs it immediately on a new thread","Returns a coroutine object that does not run until awaited or scheduled","Runs it to completion and returns the result","Raises a SyntaxError"],"a":1,"w":"Forgetting await is a classic bug: Python warns that the coroutine was never awaited, and the work never happens."},{"n":0.2,"ti":"Async Python & the Event Loop","f":"p2-async-python.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"An async def handler calls time.sleep(0.1) . Ten requests arrive at once. Roughly how long until the last one finishes?","o":["About 0.1 s","About 0.25 s","About 0.01 s","About 1 s"],"a":3,"w":"time.sleep blocks the event loop, so the requests run one after another: 10 × 0.1 s. Measured: 1.03 s."},{"n":0.2,"ti":"Async Python & the Event Loop","f":"p2-async-python.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"How does FastAPI run a handler declared with plain def ?","o":["In a thread pool, so blocking only ties up one thread","On the event loop, like an async handler","In a new process per request","It refuses: FastAPI requires async def"],"a":0,"w":"The pool has 40 threads by default, so more than 40 simultaneous blocking requests start to queue."},{"n":0.2,"ti":"Async Python & the Event Loop","f":"p2-async-python.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"Which change speeds up a handler that awaits two independent database queries one after the other?","o":["Changing it to plain def","Adding time.sleep(0) between them","Running them together with asyncio.gather","Nothing; awaits are always sequential"],"a":2,"w":"gather starts both and waits for both, so the total is about the slower of the two rather than their sum."},{"n":0.2,"ti":"Async Python & the Event Loop","f":"p2-async-python.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"A handler spends 50 ms hashing a password in pure Python. What helps most under load?","o":["Making it async def","Running the hashing in a process pool or with more worker processes","Adding await before the hash call","Increasing the thread pool to 1,000 threads"],"a":1,"w":"CPU-bound Python is serialised by the event loop or the GIL. Separate processes can use separate cores. (Real password hashing libraries are C code that may release the GIL.)"},{"n":0.3,"ti":"curl, HTTPie & the Browser Dev Tools","f":"p3-curl-and-http-tools.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"What Content-Type does curl -d '{\"name\":\"mug\"}' URL send?","o":["application/json","text/plain","application/x-www-form-urlencoded","None at all"],"a":2,"w":"-d is for form data. FastAPI then rejects a JSON body parameter with 422."},{"n":0.3,"ti":"curl, HTTPie & the Browser Dev Tools","f":"p3-curl-and-http-tools.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"curl prints nothing and exits 0 for a URL. What is the most likely explanation?","o":["The response had an empty body, for example a redirect that curl did not follow","The server was down","curl crashed","The response was gzip-compressed"],"a":0,"w":"A server that is down gives a connection error and a non-zero exit code. Use -i to see the status and the location header."},{"n":0.3,"ti":"curl, HTTPie & the Browser Dev Tools","f":"p3-curl-and-http-tools.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"Where does the -v trace go?","o":["stdout, mixed into the body","A log file","Nowhere unless -i is also set","stderr, so piping the body to another program still works"],"a":3,"w":"That is why curl -v URL | python -m json.tool works."},{"n":0.3,"ti":"curl, HTTPie & the Browser Dev Tools","f":"p3-curl-and-http-tools.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"A deploy script runs curl https://host/health and continues if curl succeeds. The service returns 500. What happens?","o":["curl exits 22 and the script stops","curl exits 0 and the script carries on","curl retries until it gets a 200","curl exits 500"],"a":1,"w":"Without -f (or --fail-with-body), an HTTP error is still a successful transfer as far as curl is concerned."},{"n":0.3,"ti":"curl, HTTPie & the Browser Dev Tools","f":"p3-curl-and-http-tools.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"In HTTPie, what does price:=8.5 send?","o":["A header named price","The string \"8.5\"","The JSON number 8.5","A query parameter"],"a":2,"w":"= sends a string, := a raw JSON value, and Name:value a header."},{"n":0.4,"ti":"JSON, Schemas & Serialisation","f":"p4-json-and-schemas.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"In Pydantic's default (lax) mode, what happens to {\"v\": true} for a field v: int ?","o":["422: a boolean is not an integer","It is accepted as 1","It is accepted as True","It is accepted as 0"],"a":1,"w":"Measured. Strict mode rejects it with int_type."},{"n":0.4,"ti":"JSON, Schemas & Serialisation","f":"p4-json-and-schemas.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"A JavaScript client parses {\"id\": 9007199254740993} . What does it get?","o":["An error","The exact number","A string","A nearby number, silently rounded"],"a":3,"w":"Doubles represent integers exactly only up to 2^53, so the ID becomes 9007199254740992. Send large IDs as strings."},{"n":0.4,"ti":"JSON, Schemas & Serialisation","f":"p4-json-and-schemas.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"What does json.dumps({\"x\": float(\"nan\")}) return in Python?","o":["{\"x\": NaN} , which is not valid JSON","{\"x\": null}","A ValueError","{\"x\": \"nan\"}"],"a":0,"w":"Pass allow_nan=False to get an error instead of invalid output."},{"n":0.4,"ti":"JSON, Schemas & Serialisation","f":"p4-json-and-schemas.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"In a PATCH request, what is the difference between {\"phone\": null} and {} ?","o":["None: both leave the phone number unchanged","None: both remove the phone number","The first says \"clear the phone number\", the second says nothing about it","The first is invalid JSON"],"a":2,"w":"Pydantic records which fields were actually sent in model_fields_set; model_dump(exclude_unset=True) uses it."},{"n":0.4,"ti":"JSON, Schemas & Serialisation","f":"p4-json-and-schemas.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"A client sends a field your Pydantic model does not declare. With the default configuration, what happens?","o":["422 with extra_forbidden","It is silently ignored","It is added to the model as an attribute","The request is logged and rejected"],"a":1,"w":"The default is extra=\"ignore\". Use extra=\"forbid\" to reject unknown fields, which also catches typos."},{"n":0.5,"ti":"SQL for Application Developers","f":"p5-sql-for-app-developers.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"Which query can an index on (customer_id, created_at) serve without any sort?","o":["WHERE created_at >= ?","WHERE status = 'pending'","WHERE customer_id = ? ORDER BY created_at DESC LIMIT 20","ORDER BY total_cents"],"a":2,"w":"Within one customer the index is already in date order, so the database reads the newest 20 entries directly."},{"n":0.5,"ti":"SQL for Application Developers","f":"p5-sql-for-app-developers.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"A handler fetches 50 posts, then runs one query per post to fetch its author. What is this called and what is the fix?","o":["The N+1 problem; fetch the authors with a join or one query using IN","A full scan; add an index on posts","SQL injection; use parameters","A deadlock; use a transaction"],"a":0,"w":"51 round trips instead of one or two. Measured here: 101 queries took about 40 times as long as one join, before any network latency."},{"n":0.5,"ti":"SQL for Application Developers","f":"p5-sql-for-app-developers.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"Why can't you pass the column in ORDER BY as a query parameter?","o":["You can; all drivers support it","Parameters only work in WHERE clauses","It would be slower","Parameters stand for values, not identifiers; check the column against an allowlist instead"],"a":3,"w":"A parameter for ORDER BY would sort by a constant string, not by the column."},{"n":0.5,"ti":"SQL for Application Developers","f":"p5-sql-for-app-developers.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"In a SQLite plan, what does SCAN orders tell you?","o":["An index on orders was used","Every row of orders is read","The query was cached","The table was locked"],"a":1,"w":"PostgreSQL calls it a Seq Scan. Fine for a small table, a warning sign for a large one on a hot path."},{"n":0.5,"ti":"SQL for Application Developers","f":"p5-sql-for-app-developers.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"You add an index on created_at , and a customer's \"latest 20 orders\" query gets slower. Why might that happen?","o":["Indexes always slow down reads","The planner walked the date index looking for that customer's rows, which cost more than a plain scan","The index was corrupted","The ORDER BY was ignored"],"a":1,"w":"Measured here: about 3 times slower than no index. The right index is (customer_id, created_at)."},{"n":0.6,"ti":"Configuration, Secrets & Environments","f":"p6-config-and-environments.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"What is bool(\"false\") in Python?","o":["False","A ValueError","True, because any non-empty string is truthy","None"],"a":2,"w":"Environment variables are strings. Let a settings library parse booleans."},{"n":0.6,"ti":"Configuration, Secrets & Environments","f":"p6-config-and-environments.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"With the loader on this page, API_KEY is in a .env file and also mounted as a secret file. Which value does the service use?","o":["The one from .env","The secret file's","Neither: it refuses to start","Whichever was modified most recently"],"a":0,"w":".env outranks secret files, as in pydantic-settings. That is why a .env must never reach a deployed image."},{"n":0.6,"ti":"Configuration, Secrets & Environments","f":"p6-config-and-environments.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"What does f\"{settings.api_key}\" produce when api_key is a SecretStr ?","o":["The real key","An error","An empty string","**********"],"a":3,"w":"Measured. Only api_key.get_secret_value() returns the real value."},{"n":0.6,"ti":"Configuration, Secrets & Environments","f":"p6-config-and-environments.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"Why validate settings at startup rather than when each one is first used?","o":["It is faster","A bad deploy fails immediately and visibly, instead of breaking later on a rarely used path","Environment variables disappear after startup","FastAPI requires it"],"a":1,"w":"A service that will not start keeps the old version running during a rolling deploy; one that starts and fails later serves errors."},{"n":0.6,"ti":"Configuration, Secrets & Environments","f":"p6-config-and-environments.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"A secret was committed and the commit was deleted an hour later. What is the essential step?","o":["Force-push to rewrite history, and nothing else","Make the repository private","Revoke and rotate the secret","Add the file to .gitignore"],"a":2,"w":"Cleaning history is good hygiene, but only rotation makes a copied secret useless."},{"n":0.7,"ti":"Testing Basics with pytest","f":"p7-pytest-basics.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"A suite reaches 100% line coverage. What does that guarantee?","o":["There are no bugs in the covered code","Every line ran during the tests, nothing more","Every branch was taken both ways","Every function's result was asserted"],"a":1,"w":"In the lab, a suite with 100% line coverage missed two of five planted bugs."},{"n":0.7,"ti":"Testing Basics with pytest","f":"p7-pytest-basics.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"How does a pytest test get a fixture called client ?","o":["By importing it from conftest","By calling client() inside the test","Through a global variable","By naming client as a parameter of the test function"],"a":3,"w":"pytest sees the parameter name, finds the fixture (in the file or a conftest.py), and passes its value."},{"n":0.7,"ti":"Testing Basics with pytest","f":"p7-pytest-basics.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"What does code after yield in a fixture do?","o":["Runs after the test, as teardown, even if the test failed","Runs before the test","Never runs","Runs only if the test passed"],"a":0,"w":"That makes yield fixtures the natural place to close connections and clear overrides."},{"n":0.7,"ti":"Testing Basics with pytest","f":"p7-pytest-basics.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"You want the same assertion checked for ten different inputs. What is the pytest way?","o":["A for loop inside one test","Ten copies of the test","@pytest.mark.parametrize","A fixture with scope=\"session\""],"a":2,"w":"Each case is reported separately, so one failure does not hide the rest, unlike a loop."},{"n":0.7,"ti":"Testing Basics with pytest","f":"p7-pytest-basics.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"How do you make a FastAPI endpoint use a fake payment client in tests?","o":["Edit the endpoint to check if it is running under pytest","Provide the client as a dependency and set app.dependency_overrides","Point the real client at a production test account","Skip the endpoint tests"],"a":1,"w":"Overrides swap dependencies without changing the endpoint's code."},{"n":0.8,"ti":"How a Request Reaches Your Code","f":"p8-request-path.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"Your service runs behind a TLS-terminating proxy. What does request.url.scheme show without proxy-header handling?","o":["https, always","Whatever the client sent in the Host header","http, because the proxy connects over plain HTTP","An error"],"a":2,"w":"The proxy reports the original scheme in X-Forwarded-Proto; uvicorn applies it with --proxy-headers from trusted addresses."},{"n":0.8,"ti":"How a Request Reaches Your Code","f":"p8-request-path.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"Why should a service only trust X-Forwarded-For from its own proxy?","o":["Any client can send the header, choosing whatever IP address it likes","The header is encrypted","Other proxies use a different format","It slows down requests"],"a":0,"w":"Rate limits, logs and IP allowlists would otherwise use an address the attacker picked."},{"n":0.8,"ti":"How a Request Reaches Your Code","f":"p8-request-path.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"A client 90 ms away opens a new HTTPS connection (TLS 1.3) for each request. Roughly how many round trips before the first response byte?","o":["One","Two","Five","Three: TCP, TLS, then the request"],"a":3,"w":"About 270 ms of waiting before any server time. With a reused connection, only the request's round trip remains."},{"n":0.8,"ti":"How a Request Reaches Your Code","f":"p8-request-path.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"What does running uvicorn with --workers 4 do?","o":["Four threads share one event loop","Four separate processes, each with its own event loop and memory","Four event loops in one process, sharing memory","Handles four requests at a time in total"],"a":1,"w":"Processes share nothing in memory, so shared state goes in a database or Redis."},{"n":0.8,"ti":"How a Request Reaches Your Code","f":"p8-request-path.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"In the lab, which stage inside FastAPI grew most when the response went from 1 item to 1,000?","o":["Routing","Dependencies","Serialisation","The handler"],"a":2,"w":"Each item is validated against the response model and turned into JSON; the handler only sliced a list."},{"n":1,"ti":"What a Backend Does","f":"01-what-a-backend-does.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"A counter is stored in a Python dictionary, and the service runs with three worker processes. After 300 increments, what does a read return?","o":["300 from every worker","An error","That worker's own share, so different reads disagree","900"],"a":2,"w":"Measured: the three workers answered 116, 95 and 89, which add up to 300."},{"n":1,"ti":"What a Backend Does","f":"01-what-a-backend-does.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"What happens to values held in a service's process memory when it is redeployed?","o":["They are lost: the new processes start empty","They are copied to the new version","They are written to the database automatically","They survive if the code did not change"],"a":0,"w":"That is why state belongs in a shared store that outlives every process."},{"n":1,"ti":"What a Backend Does","f":"01-what-a-backend-does.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"Roughly how much downtime does 99.9% availability allow in a 30-day month?","o":["About 4 minutes","About 7 hours","About 26 seconds","About 43 minutes"],"a":3,"w":"0.1% of 43,200 minutes. 99.99% allows about 4.3 minutes."},{"n":1,"ti":"What a Backend Does","f":"01-what-a-backend-does.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"Why report p99 latency rather than the average?","o":["p99 is always lower","The average hides the slow requests that many users meet, especially on pages making many calls","Averages cannot be computed for latency","p99 measures availability"],"a":1,"w":"A page making 20 calls hits at least one request slower than p99 about 18% of the time."},{"n":1,"ti":"What a Backend Does","f":"01-what-a-backend-does.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"What does Hyrum's law say about an API with many users?","o":["It should be versioned every year","It will eventually need GraphQL","Every observable behaviour will be depended on by somebody","Its latency doubles with each new user"],"a":2,"w":"So \"undocumented\" behaviour, such as field order or error text, is still part of the contract in practice."},{"n":2,"ti":"HTTP for API Builders","f":"02-http-in-depth.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"Which methods are idempotent?","o":["POST and PATCH","GET, HEAD, PUT and DELETE","Only GET","All of them"],"a":1,"w":"Sending them several times has the same effect as once, so retries are safe. OPTIONS is idempotent too."},{"n":2,"ti":"HTTP for API Builders","f":"02-http-in-depth.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"A FastAPI app declares only @app.get(\"/items/{id}\") . What does HEAD /items/1 return?","o":["200 with headers only","204","404","405 Method Not Allowed"],"a":3,"w":"Measured. Declare HEAD explicitly with api_route if you need it."},{"n":2,"ti":"HTTP for API Builders","f":"02-http-in-depth.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"A client sends If-None-Match with the ETag it already has, and the resource has not changed. What is the best response?","o":["304 Not Modified, with no body","200 with the full body","412 Precondition Failed","204 No Content"],"a":0,"w":"412 is for If-Match failing on an update; 304 is for an unchanged GET."},{"n":2,"ti":"HTTP for API Builders","f":"02-http-in-depth.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"A request is authenticated, but the user may not see this resource. Which status fits?","o":["401 Unauthorized","400 Bad Request","403 Forbidden, or 404 if even its existence is private","500"],"a":2,"w":"401 means \"who are you?\": missing or invalid credentials."},{"n":2,"ti":"HTTP for API Builders","f":"02-http-in-depth.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"Why must a \"delete\" action never be a GET link?","o":["GET requests cannot carry an ID","Crawlers, prefetchers and link previews follow GET links freely, because GET is defined as safe","Browsers block GET links","GET is slower than DELETE"],"a":1,"w":"Google Web Accelerator deleted users' data in 2005 by prefetching exactly such links."},{"n":3,"ti":"Your First Service with FastAPI","f":"03-first-fastapi-service.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"In @app.get(\"/shops/{shop_id}/items\") def f(shop_id: int, limit: int = 10) , where does limit come from?","o":["The path","The JSON body","The query string, optional with a default of 10","A header"],"a":2,"w":"It is a simple type not named in the path template."},{"n":3,"ti":"Your First Service with FastAPI","f":"03-first-fastapi-service.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"A request to /shops/seven/items hits a route with shop_id: int . What happens?","o":["422 with loc [\"path\", \"shop_id\"], and the handler is not called","The handler gets shop_id = \"seven\"","404","500"],"a":0,"w":"Measured: int_parsing, \"Input should be a valid integer\"."},{"n":3,"ti":"Your First Service with FastAPI","f":"03-first-fastapi-service.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"What does ?tags=kitchen,sale give a tags: list[str] = Query(default=[]) parameter?","o":["[\"kitchen\", \"sale\"]","A 422 error","An empty list","[\"kitchen,sale\"]"],"a":3,"w":"Repeat the parameter instead: ?tags=kitchen&tags=sale."},{"n":3,"ti":"Your First Service with FastAPI","f":"03-first-fastapi-service.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"A handler returns a dict containing cost_cents , and the route has response_model=ItemOut , which has no such field. What does the client receive?","o":["The full dict, including cost_cents","Only ItemOut's fields","A 500 error","A 422 error"],"a":1,"w":"The response model filters the output, which keeps internal fields private by default."},{"n":3,"ti":"Your First Service with FastAPI","f":"03-first-fastapi-service.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"A body has three invalid fields. How many errors does FastAPI report?","o":["Only the first","None: it returns 400 with a generic message","All of them, each with its location","It depends on field order"],"a":2,"w":"Measured: one 422 listing the query, name and price errors together."},{"n":4,"ti":"REST Resource Design","f":"04-rest-resource-design.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"Which URL best lists a customer's paid orders?","o":["GET /getPaidOrders?customer=7","GET /orders?customer_id=7&status=paid (or /customers/7/orders?status=paid )","POST /orders/list","GET /customers/7/orders/paid/list"],"a":1,"w":"A collection filtered with the query string."},{"n":4,"ti":"REST Resource Design","f":"04-rest-resource-design.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"How should an API start a report that takes ten minutes to build?","o":["GET that waits ten minutes","POST that returns 200 after ten minutes","PATCH on the report's status","POST that returns 202 with the Location of a job to poll"],"a":3,"w":"The job is a resource: the client polls it, or is notified by a webhook."},{"n":4,"ti":"REST Resource Design","f":"04-rest-resource-design.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"In the lab, how many requests did the linked-resources design need for a 20-line order?","o":["23","1","3","20"],"a":0,"w":"The order, its customer and its items list, then one per product. They fit in 3 rounds if sent in parallel."},{"n":4,"ti":"REST Resource Design","f":"04-rest-resource-design.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"What is the main cost of a screen-specific endpoint?","o":["More round trips","Larger responses","It ties the API to one client's layout, so screen changes need API changes","It cannot be cached"],"a":2,"w":"That is why such endpoints often live in a backend for frontend owned by the client team."},{"n":4,"ti":"REST Resource Design","f":"04-rest-resource-design.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"Why model a refund as POST /payments/9/refunds instead of PATCH /payments/9 ?","o":["PATCH is not allowed on payments","A refund has its own identity, status and history, and can happen more than once","POST is faster","It avoids authentication"],"a":1,"w":"As a resource, refunds can be listed, fetched and audited like anything else."},{"n":5,"ti":"Validation & Error Responses","f":"05-validation-and-errors.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"With FastAPI's defaults, what does a client receive when a handler raises an exception nobody handles?","o":["A JSON 500 with the exception message","A 422","A plain-text 500 saying \"Internal Server Error\"","The connection is dropped"],"a":2,"w":"Measured: text/plain, 21 bytes. Not JSON, so clients that always parse JSON fail again."},{"n":5,"ti":"Validation & Error Responses","f":"05-validation-and-errors.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"Which status fits an order for a product that is out of stock?","o":["409 Conflict, with a code such as out_of_stock","500","400","200 with \"error\": \"out of stock\""],"a":0,"w":"The request is valid; it conflicts with the current state of the stock."},{"n":5,"ti":"Validation & Error Responses","f":"05-validation-and-errors.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"Why give errors a stable code as well as a message?","o":["Codes are shorter","HTTP requires it","Messages are not allowed in JSON","Clients can branch on the code safely, while messages get reworded and translated"],"a":3,"w":"The code is part of the contract; the message is for people."},{"n":5,"ti":"Validation & Error Responses","f":"05-validation-and-errors.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"What did FastAPI(debug=True) send for an exception whose message contained a database URL?","o":["A generic 500","The full traceback, including the URL and its password","A 503","Nothing: debug mode only affects logging"],"a":1,"w":"About 4 KB of traceback, sent to whoever made the request."},{"n":5,"ti":"Validation & Error Responses","f":"05-validation-and-errors.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"What is the media type of an RFC 9457 error response?","o":["application/json+error","text/problem","application/problem+json","application/error"],"a":2,"w":"Its standard members are type, title, status, detail and instance; you may add your own, such as code."},{"n":6,"ti":"Talking to a Database","f":"06-database-access.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"When does a SQLAlchemy Session give its connection back to the pool?","o":["After each query","On commit, rollback or close","Only when the process exits","Never; the pool takes it back after a timeout"],"a":1,"w":"So a session that stays in a transaction keeps its connection checked out."},{"n":6,"ti":"Talking to a Database","f":"06-database-access.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"In the lab, what happened when handlers held their connection through a 200 ms provider call, with a pool of 5?","o":["Requests failed immediately","Nothing changed","The database crashed","Only five calls ran at once, so the burst took about four times longer"],"a":3,"w":"Measured: 2.5 s instead of 0.6 s for 60 requests."},{"n":6,"ti":"Talking to a Database","f":"06-database-access.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"A service runs 8 containers with 4 workers each, every worker with pool_size=5 and max_overflow=10. How many connections can it open?","o":["480","15","60","160"],"a":0,"w":"32 processes × 15. Far above PostgreSQL's default limit of 100."},{"n":6,"ti":"Talking to a Database","f":"06-database-access.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"What does pool_pre_ping=True protect against?","o":["SQL injection","Pool exhaustion","Lending out a connection the database or network has silently dropped","Slow queries"],"a":2,"w":"It checks the connection before handing it over and replaces it if it is dead."},{"n":6,"ti":"Talking to a Database","f":"06-database-access.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"Why should pool_timeout be shorter than the request timeout?","o":["To save memory","So requests fail fast with a clear error instead of waiting after the client has given up","It must, or SQLAlchemy refuses to start","To make connections open faster"],"a":1,"w":"Waiting requests also hold threads, which spreads the problem."},{"n":7,"ti":"ORMs, Migrations & N+1 Queries","f":"07-orms-and-migrations.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"Code loads 50 orders, then reads order.customer.name and order.lines for each, with default lazy loading. Roughly how many queries run?","o":["1","3","About 100","2,500"],"a":2,"w":"Measured: 100. One for the orders, then customers (minus repeats) and lines per order."},{"n":7,"ti":"ORMs, Migrations & N+1 Queries","f":"07-orms-and-migrations.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"Which loading strategy usually suits a one-to-many collection such as an order's lines?","o":["selectinload","lazy","joinedload, always","raiseload"],"a":0,"w":"One extra IN query, without multiplying parent rows the way a JOIN does."},{"n":7,"ti":"ORMs, Migrations & N+1 Queries","f":"07-orms-and-migrations.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"You rename a model attribute and run alembic revision --autogenerate . What does the draft do?","o":["Renames the column","Nothing","Asks whether it is a rename","Drops the old column and adds a new, empty one"],"a":3,"w":"Which is why every draft must be read before it is committed."},{"n":7,"ti":"ORMs, Migrations & N+1 Queries","f":"07-orms-and-migrations.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"What is raiseload for?","o":["Making queries faster","Turning an accidental lazy load into an error, so N+1s are caught in tests","Raising the pool size","Retrying failed queries"],"a":1,"w":"Measured: 'Order.customer' is not available due to lazy='raise'."},{"n":7,"ti":"ORMs, Migrations & N+1 Queries","f":"07-orms-and-migrations.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"Where should migrations run in production?","o":["At startup in every worker process","Manually from a developer's laptop","As one deploy step, once, before or alongside the release","Never; edit the schema by hand"],"a":2,"w":"Running from every worker risks several processes racing to apply the same change."},{"n":8,"ti":"Pagination, Filtering & Sorting","f":"08-pagination-filtering.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"Why does LIMIT 50 OFFSET 500000 get slower as the offset grows, even with an index?","o":["The index is not used","The database still steps past every skipped entry before returning 50","LIMIT is ignored with large offsets","It sorts the whole table each time"],"a":1,"w":"Measured: about 81 ms at page 19,001 against about 0.24 ms for page 1."},{"n":8,"ti":"Pagination, Filtering & Sorting","f":"08-pagination-filtering.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"A client pages with offsets while three new orders are created at the top. What happens on the next page?","o":["Nothing changes","The new orders appear on the next page","The request fails","The last three items of the previous page appear again"],"a":3,"w":"Every item shifted down three positions. Measured: orders 41, 42 and 43 repeated."},{"n":8,"ti":"Pagination, Filtering & Sorting","f":"08-pagination-filtering.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"Why add id to ORDER BY created_at DESC ?","o":["To make the order total, so tied timestamps cannot straddle pages unpredictably","To make the query faster","IDs are required by SQL in ORDER BY","To hide the timestamp"],"a":0,"w":"And the index should be on (created_at, id) in the same order."},{"n":8,"ti":"Pagination, Filtering & Sorting","f":"08-pagination-filtering.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"How should a list endpoint accept a client-chosen sort field?","o":["As a query parameter passed straight into ORDER BY","As a SQL parameter placeholder","Through an allowlist mapping names to known columns","It should never allow sorting"],"a":2,"w":"Placeholders only work for values, not identifiers."},{"n":8,"ti":"Pagination, Filtering & Sorting","f":"08-pagination-filtering.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"What is the cheapest way to tell a client whether another page exists?","o":["Run COUNT(*) on every request","Fetch one row more than the limit and check whether it came back","Return the total from a cache that never expires","Always return next_cursor"],"a":1,"w":"The extra row costs almost nothing; a count on a big table costs tens of milliseconds or more."}];
